WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.643
In this lesson you'll learn how a path

2
00:00:03.643 --> 00:00:06.151
points to a file, and why the same

3
00:00:06.151 --> 00:00:08.009
name can point somewhere different

4
00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:10.760
depending on where a program was started.

5
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.153
A path is an address,

6
00:00:12.153 --> 00:00:15.230
and a relative path only makes sense

7
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.018
from the working directory

8
00:00:17.018 --> 00:00:19.329
of the program that reads it.

9
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:21.140
Nobody handed you this map.

10
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.276
That's not a character flaw.

11
00:00:23.276 --> 00:00:25.099
It's a missing semester.

12
00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:27.154
Aaron, who made this course,

13
00:00:27.154 --> 00:00:30.091
had an IT degree and still didn't know to open

14
00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:33.760
a terminal and cd into the folder first.

15
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.501
So here's the shelf to put this on.

16
00:00:36.951 --> 00:00:40.829
This lesson sits in District One, The Machine.

17
00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:43.093
That district answers one question:

18
00:00:43.093 --> 00:00:44.859
what's on your computer?

19
00:00:45.322 --> 00:00:48.724
Module 01 is about how that stuff is laid out.

20
00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:51.521
Every file on your computer has an address,

21
00:00:51.521 --> 00:00:54.250
a line of text that says where it is.

22
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.620
A path is that line of text.

23
00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.682
Picture a map drawn on the disk.

24
00:00:59.682 --> 00:01:03.641
Its edge is the root, where every address begins.

25
00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:06.660
On Windows, each drive has its own root,

26
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.469
so there is one edge per drive.

27
00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:12.233
Now picture giving directions on that map:

28
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:15.263
three streets east, then two up.

29
00:01:15.263 --> 00:01:18.166
Those directions only work from one spot.

30
00:01:18.166 --> 00:01:20.778
So put a you-are-here dot on the map.

31
00:01:20.778 --> 00:01:23.274
That dot is the working directory.

32
00:01:23.274 --> 00:01:25.805
Every running program has one.

33
00:01:25.805 --> 00:01:27.245
Keep this picture.

34
00:01:27.245 --> 00:01:30.161
The map is going to get a second dot.

35
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.360
An absolute path is a full street address.

36
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.949
It starts at the root of the disk,

37
00:01:36.949 --> 00:01:40.444
the edge of the map, and counts from there.

38
00:01:40.444 --> 00:01:42.441
So it reads the same wherever

39
00:01:42.441 --> 00:01:44.496
the dot happens to be.

40
00:01:44.496 --> 00:01:48.164
On Windows, the root begins with a drive letter,

41
00:01:48.164 --> 00:01:51.171
so you'll see C:\,

42
00:01:51.171 --> 00:01:53.958
then folder names, then the file.

43
00:01:53.958 --> 00:01:55.664
On macOS and Linux,

44
00:01:55.664 --> 00:01:58.927
the root is a single forward slash.

45
00:01:58.927 --> 00:02:01.620
Microsoft's own wording is that a full

46
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.511
path starts from a drive or a root.

47
00:02:04.511 --> 00:02:07.170
The backslash is the Windows habit,

48
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.688
and the forward slash is the Unix habit.

49
00:02:10.688 --> 00:02:14.148
Windows generally accepts forward slashes too,

50
00:02:14.148 --> 00:02:16.133
and so does PowerShell.

51
00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:19.384
The separator is a naming convention.

52
00:02:19.384 --> 00:02:23.530
The operating system decides what the name means.

53
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.173
A relative path leaves out the start.

54
00:02:27.173 --> 00:02:29.495
It says three streets from here,

55
00:02:29.495 --> 00:02:32.525
and here means the working directory.

56
00:02:32.525 --> 00:02:36.089
Every running program has a working directory.

57
00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.945
It is set when the program starts,

58
00:02:38.945 --> 00:02:42.904
and it is inherited from whatever started it.

59
00:02:42.904 --> 00:02:44.297
If you typed the command,

60
00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.049
the place you typed it became the dot.

61
00:02:47.049 --> 00:02:51.031
A relative path is measured from that directory.

62
00:02:51.031 --> 00:02:53.098
Not from the file that contains it,

63
00:02:53.098 --> 00:02:55.688
and not from the root of the disk.

64
00:02:56.151 --> 00:02:59.193
Many terminals show the dot in the prompt.

65
00:02:59.193 --> 00:03:01.039
PowerShell's default prompt looks

66
00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:04.046
like PSC colon backslash,

67
00:03:04.046 --> 00:03:07.146
then your folder, then a greater-than sign.

68
00:03:07.146 --> 00:03:08.771
That prompt is the dot,

69
00:03:08.771 --> 00:03:10.838
printed on the screen for you.

70
00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:14.113
Read it before you trust any relative name.

71
00:03:14.564 --> 00:03:18.314
Here is where "it works on my machine" comes from.

72
00:03:18.314 --> 00:03:20.427
Imagine a script that opens a file

73
00:03:20.427 --> 00:03:24.305
called data dot see SV, by its bare name.

74
00:03:24.305 --> 00:03:26.290
Run it from the project folder,

75
00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.438
and the name finds the file.

76
00:03:28.438 --> 00:03:30.110
The script works.

77
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.979
Run it from another folder,

78
00:03:31.979 --> 00:03:35.299
and the same name looks in that folder,

79
00:03:35.299 --> 00:03:37.610
finds nothing, and fails.

80
00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.177
The code did not change.

81
00:03:39.177 --> 00:03:41.011
The dot moved.

82
00:03:41.011 --> 00:03:44.378
Your friend's dot sits on their own project,

83
00:03:44.378 --> 00:03:46.399
so for them it works.

84
00:03:46.399 --> 00:03:49.591
Both of you are right about your own dot.

85
00:03:49.591 --> 00:03:52.413
When a relative name fails, check where the

86
00:03:52.413 --> 00:03:55.595
program was standing before you check the file.

87
00:03:56.045 --> 00:03:58.518
Three shortcuts show up in paths.

88
00:03:58.518 --> 00:04:00.062
Tilde means your home folder,

89
00:04:00.062 --> 00:04:02.709
and the shell expands it for you.

90
00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.207
On Windows, that is usually

91
00:04:04.207 --> 00:04:08.886
something like C:\Users and your name.

92
00:04:08.886 --> 00:04:11.405
A single dot means this folder.

93
00:04:11.405 --> 00:04:14.888
Two dots mean the folder one step up.

94
00:04:14.888 --> 00:04:17.895
You only need to recognize them for now.

95
00:04:17.895 --> 00:04:19.892
Now the place where the picture breaks.

96
00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.144
Real people carry their own dot.

97
00:04:22.144 --> 00:04:24.617
A computer's dot is a stored value

98
00:04:24.617 --> 00:04:27.183
inside each running program.

99
00:04:27.183 --> 00:04:29.435
When one program starts another,

100
00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.976
the child gets a copy, not a shared pointer.

101
00:04:32.976 --> 00:04:36.482
So there are many copies, one per running program.

102
00:04:36.482 --> 00:04:40.035
And the top of the disk has nowhere further to go.

103
00:04:40.035 --> 00:04:43.971
Two dots at the root stay at the root.

104
00:04:43.971 --> 00:04:47.804
Don't stretch the metaphor past that edge.

105
00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:51.296
So what does a cd actually do?

106
00:04:51.296 --> 00:04:54.372
It changes the dot for the session that ran it,

107
00:04:54.372 --> 00:04:56.276
and only that session.

108
00:04:56.276 --> 00:04:59.400
A child process cannot move its parent.

109
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.453
If a program changes its own folder,

110
00:05:02.453 --> 00:05:06.005
the window you typed into keeps its own dot.

111
00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:07.538
That is the general rule,

112
00:05:07.538 --> 00:05:11.137
for every program that keeps a working directory.

113
00:05:11.137 --> 00:05:14.666
Python is the clearest case of a second list.

114
00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:17.836
Other tools have their own lookups too.

115
00:05:17.836 --> 00:05:21.226
Node, for example, resolves a required file

116
00:05:21.226 --> 00:05:24.395
from the calling file's folder, not the shell's.

117
00:05:24.395 --> 00:05:27.158
When you run python script .py,

118
00:05:27.158 --> 00:05:30.165
the import list starts with that script's folder.

119
00:05:30.165 --> 00:05:32.360
With dash M or dash C,

120
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.809
it starts with the current folder.

121
00:05:34.809 --> 00:05:37.479
Python 3.11 added dash P

122
00:05:37.479 --> 00:05:42.019
and an environment variable, PYTHON safe path,

123
00:05:42.019 --> 00:05:45.293
which stop Python putting those folders first.

124
00:05:45.293 --> 00:05:48.613
That story belongs to 05.02.

125
00:05:48.613 --> 00:05:50.761
A file path and an import path

126
00:05:50.761 --> 00:05:52.829
are two different lookups.

127
00:05:53.281 --> 00:05:55.998
On macOS, on Linux, and in Git Bash,

128
00:05:55.998 --> 00:05:59.957
you'll often see: no such file or directory.

129
00:05:59.957 --> 00:06:04.624
Read it as: this name is not found from this dot.

130
00:06:04.624 --> 00:06:07.097
Python is more specific.

131
00:06:07.097 --> 00:06:08.606
You'll see a line like:

132
00:06:08.606 --> 00:06:13.018
FileNotFoundError, then bracketed err-no two,

133
00:06:13.018 --> 00:06:15.363
then no such file or directory,

134
00:06:15.363 --> 00:06:19.404
then the name in quotes, data dot see SV.

135
00:06:19.404 --> 00:06:20.820
The name is right.

136
00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.863
The place is wrong.

137
00:06:22.863 --> 00:06:25.243
PowerShell usually says something like:

138
00:06:25.243 --> 00:06:28.854
cannot find path, because it does not exist.

139
00:06:28.854 --> 00:06:31.327
Check the exact words on your own screen,

140
00:06:31.327 --> 00:06:33.567
because they change with the command.

141
00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:40.557
In Git Bash, C:\Users shows up as /c/Users.

142
00:06:40.557 --> 00:06:42.983
Same place, another spelling.

143
00:06:42.983 --> 00:06:45.015
If you use Claude Code,

144
00:06:45.015 --> 00:06:46.850
a tool result may end with:

145
00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:52.109
Shell cwd was reset to, and then a folder.

146
00:06:52.109 --> 00:06:53.641
Some people report seeing

147
00:06:53.641 --> 00:06:57.148
that after a cd left the project,

148
00:06:57.148 --> 00:07:00.598
and the shell went back to the project folder.

149
00:07:01.047 --> 00:07:03.787
Five years ago, in 2021,

150
00:07:03.787 --> 00:07:07.595
no mainstream coding agent ran commands for you.

151
00:07:07.595 --> 00:07:10.300
The person at the keyboard typed every cd,

152
00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.075
and only that person moved the dot.

153
00:07:13.075 --> 00:07:15.722
Claude Code opened as a limited research

154
00:07:15.722 --> 00:07:19.356
preview in the terminal in February 2025.

155
00:07:19.356 --> 00:07:23.826
Codex CLI followed in April 2025.

156
00:07:23.826 --> 00:07:26.183
Now a coding agent runs commands

157
00:07:26.183 --> 00:07:28.598
from a working directory it inherited,

158
00:07:28.598 --> 00:07:31.187
and it types the cd itself.

159
00:07:31.187 --> 00:07:33.625
Claude Code's shell keeps its folder

160
00:07:33.625 --> 00:07:35.877
from one command to the next.

161
00:07:35.877 --> 00:07:39.093
Users have reported that a cd out of the project,

162
00:07:39.093 --> 00:07:41.322
or out of an added directory,

163
00:07:41.322 --> 00:07:43.853
gets pulled back to the project folder,

164
00:07:43.853 --> 00:07:45.804
and the result says so.

165
00:07:45.804 --> 00:07:48.985
Subagents don't carry a cd over.

166
00:07:48.985 --> 00:07:52.793
The sandbox runs on macOS, Linux,

167
00:07:52.793 --> 00:07:56.287
and Windows Subsystem for Linux version two,

168
00:07:56.287 --> 00:07:58.633
per the docs read this month.

169
00:07:58.633 --> 00:08:00.746
Native Windows runs commands without

170
00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:03.137
that sandbox, so on Windows,

171
00:08:03.137 --> 00:08:05.993
the folder rule is the one that matters.

172
00:08:05.993 --> 00:08:08.652
Some people have reported the folder

173
00:08:08.652 --> 00:08:11.125
resetting between every command.

174
00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:13.888
That is a reported issue, not a rule.

175
00:08:13.888 --> 00:08:16.326
A worktree is a second folder

176
00:08:16.326 --> 00:08:19.669
for the same project, on its own branch,

177
00:08:19.669 --> 00:08:21.504
sharing one history.

178
00:08:21.504 --> 00:08:24.000
The detail is 08.11.

179
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.554
Agents can resolve paths for you

180
00:08:26.554 --> 00:08:28.017
when they know the folder.

181
00:08:28.017 --> 00:08:29.016
You still need to know

182
00:08:29.016 --> 00:08:30.572
which folder they're standing in,

183
00:08:30.572 --> 00:08:32.789
and whether it's the project you think.

184
00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:35.076
Where this goes next is a guess.

185
00:08:35.076 --> 00:08:37.340
Agents will probably do more of the typing,

186
00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.896
and the question will still be the same.

187
00:08:40.346 --> 00:08:42.437
You can skip these for now.

188
00:08:43.028 --> 00:08:47.335
Windows paths have a 260-character default.

189
00:08:47.335 --> 00:08:50.273
Apps have to opt in before that limit lifts,

190
00:08:50.273 --> 00:08:52.420
and it takes both a system setting

191
00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:54.940
and the app's own manifest.

192
00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:58.028
That's module one, lesson eight.

193
00:08:58.028 --> 00:09:00.733
Forward slashes mostly work on Windows,

194
00:09:00.733 --> 00:09:02.649
so you don't need to fight them.

195
00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:05.075
WSL reaches your C drive

196
00:09:05.075 --> 00:09:08.128
under slash mount slash C.

197
00:09:08.128 --> 00:09:12.772
Python's dash P option and PYTHON safe path

198
00:09:12.772 --> 00:09:15.977
are module five, lesson two's story.

199
00:09:15.977 --> 00:09:18.637
You don't need to memorize any of this today.

200
00:09:19.088 --> 00:09:21.410
This connects to five shelves.

201
00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.124
Hidden files, like .git and .venv,

202
00:09:26.124 --> 00:09:28.422
are paths with a dot in front.

203
00:09:28.422 --> 00:09:32.219
That's module one, lesson four.

204
00:09:32.219 --> 00:09:35.899
A bare name like python, with no folder at all,

205
00:09:35.899 --> 00:09:38.465
is found a different way, through PATH.

206
00:09:38.465 --> 00:09:41.658
That's module one, lesson five.

207
00:09:41.658 --> 00:09:44.920
Moving around with cd, ls,

208
00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:48.589
and the prompt is module two, lesson three.

209
00:09:48.589 --> 00:09:50.795
Running a file, and the imports it makes,

210
00:09:50.795 --> 00:09:53.535
is module five, lesson two.

211
00:09:53.535 --> 00:09:55.868
And a second folder for the same project,

212
00:09:55.868 --> 00:09:59.456
on its own branch, is a worktree on git.

213
00:09:59.456 --> 00:10:02.673
That's module eight, lesson eleven.

214
00:10:03.123 --> 00:10:04.656
Keep this.

215
00:10:04.656 --> 00:10:06.780
One: a path is an address.

216
00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.265
An absolute path starts at the root,

217
00:10:09.265 --> 00:10:10.797
and a relative path starts

218
00:10:10.797 --> 00:10:12.933
where the program stands.

219
00:10:12.933 --> 00:10:15.023
Two: every running program

220
00:10:15.023 --> 00:10:17.067
has a working directory.

221
00:10:17.067 --> 00:10:20.282
It is inherited from whatever started the program,

222
00:10:20.282 --> 00:10:24.183
and a cd moves only the session that ran it.

223
00:10:24.183 --> 00:10:27.190
Three: the same name can mean a different file.

224
00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.827
When something isn't found,

225
00:10:28.827 --> 00:10:31.068
check the folder the program is in.

226
00:10:31.068 --> 00:10:34.773
With an agent, check which project it's in too.
